  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/f5678237a4e3448f737500d08159b916317940cd427be6c9d65e5647fb007068.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above shows three collinear points?",
  "worked_solution": "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/64de4db5f989920ef14340ff7e218fbad9460ae836f2e4c50450b30fc2867f07.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/013d1ac170724461fd35cc1d29a4ce455890537543b362c2e9f193c3f2a92df9.png)\n\n- Diagram III shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/60489a11b93b0f55965251fc739a77dee62a962519143fa0b17d53c0e35200b2.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
